    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "This is the law that makes ZZ a group and the one that fails for the naive representation. On the raw pair (a,b) meaning a-b, commutativity and associativity of addition hold structurally, but (a,b)+(b,a) = (a+b, b+a), which is not the pair (0,0) -- so an additive inverse exists only after quotienting. Taking that quotient in this kernel would admit `Quot`/`Quot.sound` as `Declaration::Quotient`, putting `Quot.sound` in this fact's footprint permanently. The normalized `Int.ofNat`/`Int.negSucc` representation avoids it, which is why `axiom_footprint: []` is available here at all. The proof rests on `Nat.sub_self` (not itself in this ledger): `Int.subNatNat n n` puts `Nat.sub n n` in both its scrutinee and its non-negative value, so one rewrite collapses both. formal.statement is `Kernel::render_lean` of the admitted type, taken verbatim from `int_theorem_inventory`; it was not transcribed by hand.",
